.TH OSS 1 "July 2026" "oss 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
oss \- pick Linux hardening settings, review the diff, apply once
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oss
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oss
opens a GTK4 window listing hardening settings for the running system, grouped
into sections that each correspond to one configuration file. Settings are
staged without privilege; nothing is written until you review the resulting
file diff and confirm.
.PP
Applying asks for authentication once, through polkit, and hands the staged
selection to a privileged helper at
.IR /usr/libexec/oss\-helper .
The helper re\-renders every target file from its own copy of the catalogue and
accepts only setting identifiers and values, each checked against that
catalogue's allowlist.
.PP
Changes are made as a single transaction. Every file touched is backed up
first, candidate files are checked with the relevant validator where one exists
.RB ( "sshd \-t" ", " "visudo \-c" ", " "auditctl \-R" ),
and any failure rolls the whole set back. After applying, each value is read
back off the live system and reported as in effect, awaiting a reboot, or not
taken.
.SH OPTIONS
.B oss
takes no command line options. Everything is driven from the interface.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/oss/catalogue
Setting definitions, one TOML file per section.
.TP
.I /usr/share/oss/presets
Shipped presets.
.TP
.I ~/.config/oss
User settings and saved presets.
.TP
.I /var/lib/oss
Root\-owned state: the apply log and the permission rollback journal.
.TP
.I /etc/*/99\-oss.*
The drop\-in files this application writes. Deleting one and re\-running that
section's reload command returns it to stock.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OSS_DATA
Override the directory holding the catalogue, presets and themes.
.TP
.B OSS_ROOT
Redirect all writes below this directory. Honoured only when not running as
root, so it cannot be used to misdirect a privileged apply. Intended for tests.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on a clean exit.
.SH BUGS
Report at
.UR https://github.com/jegly/OSS/issues
.UE .
